OK, let me make my reasoning explicit:

 * I don't really care what other people do with regard to pudding farming.
   It's a game. Pudding farmer bones are annoying but they're not as bad 
   as people who accidentally get Yeenoghu in Minetown or whatever.

 * Pudding farming allows you to get more items per turn than other 
   strategies. This was practically the only thing I said that you
   didn't attempt to contradict, so I'm going to go out on a limb here
   and assume that you actually agree with it. :)

 * A player of constant skill is more likely to ascend with an ascension 
   kit than with shoddy equipment, as much of the risk in the game is from
   assembling such a kit while still possessed of shoddy equipment, and 
   most conducts (not food conducts) make the game harder at least in part
   by preventing you from having everything you might want in the late game:
   AC, damage per hit, equipment, other people's quest artifacts, lack of 
   liches, whatever.

 * A player of constant skill can get an ascension kit earlier and with 
   less thought by pudding farming than by using other strategies. Now,
   this statement is not strictly true --- actually diving the dungeon
   quickly gets (to use your example) Rast everything he needs. But I
   doubt Rast would turn down twenty wands of teleport if he had them --- 
   I expect he would put them to good use. A dedicated pudding farmer can 
   have twenty everything of everything else before hitting the Quest.

Maybe this isn't "unbalancing" if it is based in the game. Maybe the
word choice is problematic. But I think that a lot of what makes Nethack
interesting is the challenge of collecting everything you need and dealing
with holes in your inventory caused by what monsters and random items
don't give you, and pudding farming allows you to deal with these holes
more thoroughly and with less thought than any other strategy. Farming
create monster requires getting enough mana to cast it over and over
again (and often getting the Eye, which is a quest unto itself),  and
if nothing else dealing with different monsters that might appear on
different squares around you. It is not something you can do for a
million points by hitting your paste button.

And people can do whatever they want with the game. I'm not playing 
against them! I'm playing against me! The point of my original post was
to say that despite the effect that I mention above, which I will contend
makes pudding farming different from other methods of playing the game,
there was some reasonably high baseline of skill necessary to win. You
went on to suggest that I was completely wrong, and that despite pudding
farming, there was some reasonably high baseline of skill necessary to
win. 

Pudding farming isn't evil. Murdering people is evil (and you lose
telepathy!). But if the skill required to ascend with pudding farming
is lower than the skill required to ascend without pudding farming ---
and I believe it is, but reasonable people could disagree on this,
and I don't claim to be able to prove my opinion --- then it does change
the balance of the game, and changing it merits consideration, even if 
it ultimately isn't the correct thing to do.

I hope that my being explicit means you will argue with what I said and
not what "they" say.

